OPN/SSG/ADPCM core subset. See LICENSE.

Production Makoto uses src/sound/MakotoYM2608.{hh,cc}: an attributed adaptation
of the YM2608 control layer with direct native FM and SSG output. The vendored
ym2608 class itself is restored to the pinned upstream source. The former
patched class and MakotoNativeChip wrapper live in Contrib/makoto-reference/
for standalone regression tests; they are not linked into the emulator.

Remaining lower-engine patches:
1. Value-initialize fm_operator::m_cache, giving fields a defined state before
   the normal synthesis prepare() step.
2. Safe debugger external-peek callbacks and effective FM/SSG/ADPCM register
   access. Hosts without a peek callback return FFh instead of invoking a
   potentially destructive read. A read-only SSG resampler output accessor
   remains for the preserved differential-test reference.
3. Inclusive ADPCM-B CPU transfer boundaries: write the final byte of the end
   chunk and read the final byte of the limit chunk before wrapping. Makoto V5
   on Sanyo MSX2+ and Panasonic turbo R confirms x1 END=0 writes A1 A2 A3 A4,
   while LIMIT=0 repeats B1 B2 B3 B4. Playback boundaries are unchanged.
4. Preserve an unfinished CPU RAM writer across mode changes until explicit
   ADPCM RESET. A read returns the last written buffer byte, matching physical
   V4 rows A/B; reset permits normal reads (C/D). Peeks have no side effects.
   The latch appends one serialized byte. This is a targeted transition fix,
   not a complete bus-level model. Independent YM2608-LLE experiments reproduced
   it; no LLE code is included or linked here.
5. Makoto passes one interleaved stereo buffer per FM channel; a nullptr
   skips mixing that channel for the whole buffer.
   `generate()` clocks one channel for the whole buffer before the next.
   `prepare()` runs at the start of a buffer only after a register write or
   CSM key-on. A fade during the buffer keeps being mixed until the next
   `generate()`. A channel that is quiet at that point stays silent: key-on
   and CSM happen before `generate()`. A channel whose operators are all in
   release at attenuation 0x3ff is not clocked; an unused chip stays in that
   state. `generate()` also nulls channels the caller left out of the
   channel mask (YM2608 register 0x29 bit 7 drops channels 3-5). The
   envelope counter advances once per chunk for ADPCM-A. The LFO is walked
   only for a channel that uses it, or once at the end when it is enabled
   and no channel did. Skipping mix settles feedback in constant time.
6. `adpcm_b_channel::silent()` is level 0, both pan bits clear, or a channel
   that will not advance with both interpolator accumulators already 0.
   Makoto nulls that buffer for the whole block. `clock()` still runs.
   Level and pan are read once per buffer.
7. `adpcm_a_channel::silent()` is the volume early-out (combined level at
   least 63), both pan bits clear, or a stopped channel whose accumulator
   is already 0. A stopped channel can still emit its held accumulator until
   the next clock that includes it. Makoto nulls each silent rhythm buffer
   for the whole block. `clock()` still runs. Volume and pan are read once
   per buffer, and the scaled sample is recomputed on a clock tick.

Contrib/makoto-adpcm-transfer-test.cc covers the RAM corrections.
Contrib/makoto-owned-core-test.cc compares the new control layer with the
preserved reference for 1,080,000 samples across all 16 voices and prescalers.
See doc/internal/makoto.md and the linked fork measurements for scope/limits.

New Makoto states use native openMSX control fields and four length-checked
engine blobs. The fork retains a reader for earlier packed core states; that
reader is not part of the upstream device. See the device documentation.

Build integration: source files use .cc directly for openMSX discovery. The
OPNA register class lives in ymfm_fm.h, ahead of the FM engine that uses it;
upstream's ymfm_opn.h is gone. The FM engine classes name that register class
directly, so they carry no template parameter. ymfm_opn.cc includes
ymfm_fm.ipp, which puts the engine and the register accessors it calls in one
translation unit.
